WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.210
Flying is expensive.

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.229
Why is this?

00:00:03.229 --> 00:00:07.530
Well most would say it’s because of the
cost of fuel, but that’s not entirely true.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:11.750
The Airbus a320neo can carry 6,400 gallons
of fuel.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:16.560
That’s the equivalent of 376 fully tanked
Toyota Camry’s.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.900
For every mile that the a320 flies, it burns
1.5 gallons of jet fuel.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.100
That works out to a paltry .67 miles per gallon.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:30.040
An a320 could barely fly from the Empire State
Building to Grand Central Terminal on that

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.370
gallon of fuel.

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:38.530
To fly the 213 miles from New York to DC,
that a320 needs 317 gallons of fuel.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:44.020
With that amount, our Toyota Camry could drive
from New York to LA 49 times.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.309
But, we do have to consider that a Camry only
seats five.

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:51.280
An a320 seats 154.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:57.579
So, assuming that our plane is fully loaded,
it now has a per person fuel efficiency of

00:00:57.579 --> 00:01:00.680
104.7 miles per gallon.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.320
That’s amazing!

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.490
According to the Bureau of Transport Statistics,
airlines pay on average only $1.24 per gallon

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:09.570
of fuel.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:16.452
So, flying from New York to DC, you personally
only use $2.50 worth of fuel, so why do these

00:01:16.452 --> 00:01:18.399
tickets cost upwards of $80!

00:01:18.399 --> 00:01:23.049
Well, there’s so much more that goes into
your ticket price than fuel.

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:26.719
Before we start, I’ll quickly mention that
these are all rough estimates.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:30.009
Many of these prices will vary widely through
time and there are certainly ways that airlines

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:33.829
reduce these costs, but this will give you
a general idea of what actually makes flying

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:35.469
so expensive.

00:01:35.469 --> 00:01:38.419
The first thing we can add to our ticket price
is crew costs.

00:01:38.419 --> 00:01:45.539
A pilot makes on average around $79,000 a
year which works out to about $44 per flight

00:01:45.539 --> 00:01:46.539
hour.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:51.579
We have two pilots so for our hour long flight
from New York to DC so that’s $88 to the

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:52.579
pilots.

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:58.229
A fully loaded a320 requires four flight attendants
who are each paid around $38 per flight hour

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:03.250
for a total of $152 bringing our crew costs
up to $240.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:10.289
So, with our 154 seats that works out to just
about $1.5 per person in crew costs.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:13.409
Alright, now we’re at $4 dollars.

00:02:13.409 --> 00:02:15.879
Next, we gotta pay the airports.

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:22.290
New York’s John F Kennedy Airport charges
$6.33 per thousand pounds of airplane taking

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:26.489
off, and our a320 weighs 172,000 pounds.

00:02:26.489 --> 00:02:32.180
That means we have to give $1089 dollars to
JFK or $7 per person.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.709
Dulles airport doesn’t publish their landing
fees, but we’ll assume it’s about the

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:42.310
same as JFK’s landing fees at $1005 for
our now 170,000 pound airplane.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:47.269
These fees generally include the cost of using
gates, porters, and other airport resources,

00:02:47.269 --> 00:02:50.790
and divide down to $13.5 per person.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.280
Our total is now at $17.5.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.040
Now we get to the fun part—taxes.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.329
There’s a litany of different taxes and
they all vary by country, so for the sake

00:03:00.329 --> 00:03:03.060
of simplicity we’ll just cover the ones
for the US.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:09.569
The simplest tax is the Domestic Passenger
Ticket Tax at 7.5% of the ticket price.

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:11.280
That money goes to the FAA.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.830
If you land or take off a rural airport, which
is defined as, “an airport with less than

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:19.799
100K enplanements during 2nd preceding calendar
year, and either 1) is not located within

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:23.870
75 miles of another airport with 100K+ enplanements
per year, 2) is receiving essential air service

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:27.709
subsides, or 3) is not connected by paved
roads to another airport,” they also tack

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:29.459
on another 7.5%.

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:32.989
That, of course, doesn’t apply to either
of our airports.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:39.280
The FAA also charges $4 flat for any flight
within the US, unless you go to Hawaii or

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:40.280
Alaska.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.159
Then they charge $8.70.

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:48.080
The TSA then adds a $5.60 tax called the 9/11
security fee that goes to fund the heightened

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.280
security since 9/11.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.989
Those are only just for domestic flights.

00:03:51.989 --> 00:03:56.889
If we were flying from New York to Paris,
we would also have to add on $17.70 for the

00:03:56.889 --> 00:04:03.389
International Transportation Tax, $5.50 for
the US customs user fee, $5.00 for the U.S.

00:04:03.389 --> 00:04:08.430
Animal and Plant Health Inspection Service
Fee, $7.00 for the U.S. Immigration and Naturalization

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.219
Fee, and $14 for the French Civil Aviation
Tax.

00:04:12.219 --> 00:04:16.769
So with all the taxes and fees that apply
for this short flight, we’re now at $33

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:17.769
total.

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:21.390
Now, lets remember that airplanes are expensive.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.560
Our a320 costs 107 million dollars.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.199
The service life for airplanes is not actually
determined by flying time, it’s determined

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:33.960
by something called flight cycles, which is
basically a fancy word for one flight.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.210
It doesn’t really matter how long an airplane
flies in its service life, what matters is

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:40.759
how many times it’s pressurized.

00:04:40.759 --> 00:04:44.920
This is because every time it’s pressurized,
micro cracks develop around the rivets that

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:48.040
connect the the metal plates that make up
a fuselage.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:52.780
No matter if a plane goes on a 30 minute flight
or a 9 hour flight, the wear and tear to the

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.330
plane will be the same.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:57.720
This is why large long-haul airplanes are
often older.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.639
They might only go through one flight cycle
per day while small regional jets could go

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:03.270
through eight or ten.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.250
So, back to our a320.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.449
Our plane is actually a pretty durable aircraft.

00:05:07.449 --> 00:05:10.060
It can fly up to 60,000 cycles.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:15.139
So if we divide the total price of our plane
by the maximum number of flight cycles, we

00:05:15.139 --> 00:05:21.690
get that $1783 per flight must be devoted
towards paying off the initial price of the

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:22.690
airplane.

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:25.720
Per person, that works out to $11.5 dollars.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.259
We’re now at $44.5 dollars.

00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:33.669
To keep this plane safe, airlines have to
also perform regular maintenance.

00:05:33.669 --> 00:05:40.210
For our hour long flight, an airline has to
do maintenance that will cost $646 in labor,

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:48.580
$276 in parts, $603 in inspections and component
overhauls, and another $596 in engine restoration.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:53.360
That’s $2121 total or $14 per person.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.909
We’re up to $58.5 dollars.

00:05:56.909 --> 00:06:00.530
We also have to consider the non-flying part
of flying.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:04.669
Overseeing any flight is an airline which
is a business that has costs in and of itself.

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:10.080
They need to pay administrators, executives,
software engineers, marketers, consultants,

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.889
customer service personal, public relations
agents, janitors, along with rent, benefits,

00:06:14.889 --> 00:06:16.440
travel costs, utilities, and more.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:21.229
It’s hard to put an exact number on how
much per person this works out to, but we’ll

00:06:21.229 --> 00:06:23.590
make a conservative estimate of $10.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:25.700
We’re up to $68.5.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:27.500
Alright, insurance.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:33.520
An airline will pay about $106,500 per year
for insurance for an a320 and assuming our

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:38.569
plane spends eight hours per day in the air,
$36 of this insurance is for this hour long

00:06:38.569 --> 00:06:41.000
flight, or about 25 cents per person.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.889
So, our very rough grand total is $68.75.

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:49.849
We’ll round this up to $70 since there are
various small things I didn’t cover such

00:06:49.849 --> 00:06:55.880
as hotel costs for crew members, plane storage,
apu rental, liability insurance, etc.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:00.699
That means that $10 of our plane ticket is
profit, but keep in mind that all my estimates

00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:05.020
were on the conservative end and it’s all
assuming a fully loaded airplane.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:08.479
In the end, not a ton of the money actually
goes to the airline.

00:07:08.479 --> 00:07:11.100
Back to my first line: “flying is expensive.”

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:14.550
Well, it really isn’t and it’s getting
cheaper.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:19.050
To fly in the magic metal bird across the
country from New York to L.A. in six hours

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:22.110
costs around $340 roundtrip.

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:27.270
To drive in our very fuel efficient Toyota
Camry across the country in forty hours costs

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:33.490
$340 just in fuel then an additional $300
in estimated maintenance and depreciation

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:34.490
costs.

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:39.570
In the past 30 years, the average inflation
adjusted airfare has dropped by 50% and it’s

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:40.570
still dropping.

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:46.060
In 1974, it was actually illegal for airlines
to offer a flight between New York and LA

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:52.520
for less than $1442 inflation adjusted dollars
and now you can sometimes find these flights

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.800
for less than $200 round trip.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:59.610
One of the reasons for this drop in prices
is the rise of efficient airplanes.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:05.000
Certain innovations such as composite construction,
better aerodynamics, more efficient engines,

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.479
and, perhaps most notably, winglets have driven
down the cost of flying.

00:08:09.479 --> 00:08:13.440
Here’s the one and only “Real Engineering”
to explain winglets.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.050
As you said, the airline industry is constantly
finding ways to drive down their fuel consumption.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:23.181
In the early 70's, fuel prices rocketed, and
engineers were forced to find ways to make

00:08:23.181 --> 00:08:24.710
their planes more efficient.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:27.979
One way they found was with winglets, which
are those little curly bits at the end of

00:08:27.979 --> 00:08:28.979
the wing.

00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:30.879
Let's have a quick look at how they work.

00:08:30.879 --> 00:08:34.600
Winglets were introduced to decrease induced
drag, which occurs when high-pressure air

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:39.300
below the wing begins to bleed over the edge
into the low-pressure air above.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.730
This flow creates vortices off the tips of
the wings that creates drag and reduces lift

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:46.460
which reduces the fuel economy of the plane.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:50.560
Winglets act to reduce these vortices by reducing
the airflow over the edge of the wing.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:54.630
For a full explanation and a look at the history
behind winglets, head over to my channel and

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:59.320
watch my new video which features a special
guest appearance from Wendover Productions.

